src/backend/storage/file/README

Transactional File Operations (FILEOPS)
=======================================

This directory's fileops.c implements FILEOPS: a set of filesystem
operations that participate in PostgreSQL transactions.  An ordinary
unlink() or rename() issued in the middle of a transaction is not covered by
WAL and is not reversed if the transaction aborts or the server crashes.
FILEOPS closes that gap for the filesystem mutations the server performs
outside the buffer manager.

FILEOPS is NOT used for relation files.  Creating and dropping tables,
indexes, TRUNCATE, and CREATE/DROP DATABASE|TABLESPACE at the catalog/smgr
level go through smgr and catalog/storage.c, which have zero FileOps calls.
The only in-core callers of the FileOps* API are:

  - dbcommands.c   (CREATE/ALTER/DROP DATABASE: directory + PG_VERSION,
                    ALTER DATABASE ... SET TABLESPACE)
  - tablespace.c   (CREATE/DROP TABLESPACE: the per-tablespace directory
                    and symlink; the LOCATION paths are absolute by design)
  - copydir.c      (the destination tree of a directory copy)


When each operation takes effect, and what undoes it
----------------------------------------------------

The rule: everything that CREATES or CHANGES an object takes effect at once
and is undone if the transaction aborts; DELETING an object is DEFERRED to
transaction end, so nothing is ever destroyed that a rollback would need.

  operation              takes effect      undone on abort by (*)
  ---------------------  ----------------  ------------------------------
  FileOpsCreate          now               unlink the file
  FileOpsMkdir           now               rmdir the directory
  FileOpsMkdirTree       now               remove the whole tree
  FileOpsChmod / Chown   now               restore the old mode / owner
  FileOpsTruncate        now               restore length and removed bytes
  FileOpsWrite           now               restore the overwritten bytes
  FileOpsSymlink / Link  now               unlink the link
  FileOpsRename          now               rename back
  FileOpsSetXattr /      now               restore the old value
    FileOpsRemoveXattr
  FileOpsDelete          at commit (**)    nothing to undo: not done yet
  FileOpsRmdir /         at commit (**)    nothing to undo: not done yet
    FileOpsRmdirRecursive

  (*)  at wal_level >= replica, by the operation's UNDO record: on ROLLBACK,
       ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK PREPARED (applied with the abort
       record, which carries the inverses), on a standby replaying that
       abort record, and by the startup undo phase for a transaction a crash
       left in flight.  At wal_level = minimal, by an abort-time pending op
       on ROLLBACK and ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT only (see below).
  (**) or at abort, with at_commit = false.

Each undo reverts only an object that still shows the operation's effect
(fileops_undo_apply() in fileops_undo.c), so a name reused in the meantime is
left alone.  Undo runs newest first, so an operation on a renamed file's new
name is undone before the rename.


Immediate operations
--------------------

These run when the API is called, so the file or directory can be used
within the same transaction, and each registers an abort-time undo action
that reverses it if the transaction rolls back:

  FileOpsCreate    -- create() now; UNDO/pending-delete on abort
  FileOpsMkdir     -- mkdir() now; UNDO rmdir on abort
  FileOpsMkdirTree -- mkdir() now; UNDO removes the whole tree on abort
  FileOpsChmod     -- chmod() now; UNDO restore old mode on abort
  FileOpsChown     -- chown() now; UNDO restore old uid/gid on abort
  FileOpsTruncate  -- ftruncate() now; UNDO restore old length on abort
  FileOpsSymlink   -- symlink() now; UNDO unlink on abort
  FileOpsLink      -- link() now; UNDO unlink on abort
  FileOpsRename    -- rename() now; UNDO rename back on abort
  FileOpsWrite     -- pwrite() now (WAL-logged, bounded before-image UNDO)
  FileOpsSetXattr / FileOpsRemoveXattr -- now; UNDO restore old value on abort

FileOpsRename(oldpath, newpath) renames a file or directory.  It never
replaces newpath: an existing newpath is an ERROR (ERRCODE_DUPLICATE_FILE),
as are a missing oldpath and a newpath on another filesystem, and each
changes nothing.  Undoing a replacement would need the replaced object kept
somewhere until the transaction ends, and no caller needs one.  The rename
is reversed only while newpath exists and oldpath is free.  Like the undo of
FileOpsCreate and FileOpsMkdir, that test looks at the paths alone: the
device and inode of the object are not stable across a restore, a promoted
standby, or redo that makes the object again, so ROLLBACK PREPARED on a
promoted standby could not use them.
Unlike the other immediate operations it writes and flushes its WAL and UNDO
records BEFORE the rename() call, so a crash cannot lose the old name with
nothing on record: a crash before the call is harmless, since redo makes the
rename and the loser's UNDO reverses it.


Deferred operations
-------------------

The deleting operations only record a PendingFileOp, and the filesystem is
touched at transaction end:

  FileOpsDelete    -- deferred; at_commit chooses commit vs abort
  FileOpsRmdir / FileOpsRmdirRecursive -- deferred; at_commit

FileOpsDoPendingOps(isCommit) runs the pending list at transaction end: the
at_commit=true ops fire on commit, the at_commit=false ops fire on abort.


When FILEOPS is durable: only at wal_level >= replica
-----------------------------------------------------

FILEOPS writes WAL and UNDO records ONLY when XLogIsNeeded() is true, i.e.
wal_level >= replica.  At wal_level = minimal it emits no WAL and no UNDO:

  - There is NO crash safety at minimal.  The immediate constructive ops
    mutate the filesystem with no WAL record to redo, and the only reversal
    for a crash-mid-transaction is the in-memory pendingFileOps list, which
    does not survive a crash.

  - There is NO 2PC safety at minimal.  With nothing serialized, a PREPARE'd
    transaction's file operations cannot be reproduced after a crash.

Every FILEOPS + crash-recovery + 2PC guarantee below assumes wal_level >=
replica, which is what any deployment relying on those guarantees must set.


Revert and recovery model (NO fileops_cleanup)
----------------------------------------------

There is NO fileops_cleanup() function.  The RM_FILEOPS_ID WAL resource
manager registers only rm_redo/rm_desc/rm_identify; its rm_startup and
rm_cleanup slots are NULL (see access/rmgrlist.h).  Reversal rides two
mechanisms, both on the COMMON WAL stream:

  1. Live-transaction abort (in-memory).  ROLLBACK runs
     FileOpsDoPendingOps(false), executing each constructive op's registered
     abort-time undo (delete the created file, restore the old
     mode/length/xattr, rmdir the created directory, ...).  No WAL needed;
     the in-process list has the state.

  2. Crash / async revert (common-WAL UNDO records).  Each constructive op
     also prepares an UNDO record (PrepareXactUndoData(UNDO_RMID_FILEOPS,...))
     and inserts it (InsertXactUndoData) in the same critical section as its
     forward WAL record.  On crash, a loser transaction's UNDO chain is
     applied by the cluster-wide UNDO engine's recovery pass
     (PerformUndoRecovery, at end of redo); the apply side is
     fileops_undo_apply() in fileops_undo.c, registered through the UNDO rmgr
     registry (FileopsUndoRmgrInit), NOT through a WAL rm_cleanup callback.

Deferred ops emit their forward WAL record at commit-EXECUTION time, inside
FileOpsDoPendingOps() when the op actually happens on the primary, so a
physical standby reproduces it by redo.  Their redo handlers are idempotent
(delete of an already-absent file, rmdir of an already-absent directory both
count as success).  Only the commit path is logged; an at_commit=false
delete/rmdir run during abort emits no WAL, because a standby must never
apply an aborted transaction's effect.

Redo replays each record onto the filesystem as it is now, which after a
crash may already include later operations, and so it cannot tell a name
reused later from the original.  This is a known limit of FILEOPS redo:
making FILEOPS fully crash-atomic is future work (a resource manager that
logs enough to replay against the state the record was made in).  Rename
redo converges on the primary's state as far as the paths allow:

  - oldpath absent: the rename was made already; nothing to do.
  - newpath absent: rename oldpath to newpath.
  - both present: oldpath was made again by the replay of an earlier record
    (the CREATE, MKDIR or LINK that made it since the checkpoint), or by a
    later operation, and newpath is the later state.  Remove oldpath (a
    directory only if it is empty) and keep newpath.  If oldpath is a
    non-empty directory, leave both, with a WARNING.

Renaming over newpath instead would lose data.  After a -> b, then b -> c,
then a new b, with a older than the checkpoint, redo of b -> c finds the new
b and c both present, and would put the new b in place of c.

One deliberate exception:

  SYMLINK redo is an intentional no-op.  FileOpsSymlink is used only for
  tablespace symlinks; standby/crash-recovery replay of those is owned by
  RM_TBLSPC, which applies the recovery-time layout remap.


Two-phase commit
----------------

PREPARE TRANSACTION serializes the transaction's pending FileOps into the
2PC state file so they survive to COMMIT/ROLLBACK PREPARED, including from a
different backend after a crash between PREPARE and the final decision:

  - FileOpsAtPrepare(), called from EndPrepare() between StartPrepare() and
    the END sentinel, writes one TWOPHASE_RM_FILEOPS_ID record per pending
    at_commit=true op into the durable 2PC state.

  - The FILEOPS 2PC post-commit callback performs those deferred commit-time
    ops (delete/rmdir), reusing the same per-op executor as
    FileOpsDoPendingOps().  COMMIT PREPARED thus applies them; a crash before
    it re-drives them from the durable 2PC state.

  - The immediate ops' inverses are serialized separately, as one
    FILEOPS_TWOPHASE_INVERSES record.  ROLLBACK PREPARED carries them in its
    abort record and applies them before it releases the prepared
    transaction's locks, as an ordinary abort does; COMMIT PREPARED ignores
    them.  The FILEOPS post-abort callback is therefore effectively a no-op.

  - PostPrepare_FileOps() then discards this backend's in-memory list; the
    durable 2PC state (for commit-time ops) and the UNDO chain (for abort-time
    reversal), not this backend, drive the eventual work.

Before this, PostPrepare_FileOps() freed the pending list without recording
it, so deferred commit-time ops were lost across PREPARE.  Adding the
TWOPHASE_RM_FILEOPS_ID record type bumped TWOPHASE_MAGIC (twophase.c).


How to use the API
------------------

The public API is declared in src/include/storage/fileops.h.  Call these in
place of the raw libc/syscall equivalents whenever the operation must be
transactional.

  register_delete (FileOpsCreate).  When true, the newly created file is
  registered for deletion if the transaction aborts.

  at_commit (FileOpsDelete, FileOpsRmdir, FileOpsRmdirRecursive).
  at_commit = true removes on commit (dropping state the transaction is
  logically deleting); at_commit = false removes only on abort.

FileOpsMkdirTree creates a directory that the transaction then fills by
other means (smgr for CREATE DATABASE, copydir() for FILE_COPY and ALTER
DATABASE SET TABLESPACE).  Its UNDO record and its abort-time pending op
remove the whole tree, whatever ended up in it, where FileOpsMkdir's only
rmdir() an empty directory.  For a database directory the UNDO first drops
that database's shared buffers and pending fsync requests.

The API hides platform differences: PG_O_DIRECT for unbuffered I/O,
pg_fsync() for the correct per-platform flush, and fsync_parent_path() for
directory durability on Unix.


Transaction lifecycle integration
----------------------------------

The deferred operations are driven by AM-neutral hooks that xact.c calls
(wired to the FileOps* functions in fileops_undo.c):

  FileOpsDoPendingOps(isCommit) -- executes the pending list at end of xact.
  AtSubCommit_FileOps()         -- reparents a subxact's pending ops.
  AtSubAbort_FileOps()          -- runs a subxact's abort-time ops.
  FileOpsAtPrepare()            -- serializes pending ops into 2PC state.
  PostPrepare_FileOps()         -- discards the in-memory list after PREPARE.
  FileOpsCancelPendingDelete()  -- removes a registered delete entry.
  FileOpsSync()                 -- forces durability of a path immediately.


WAL replay
----------

fileops_redo() dispatches each XLOG_FILEOPS_* record to the matching action
during recovery; fileops_desc()/fileops_identify() render records for
pg_waldump.  Because the redo handler is itself the recovery action, code
paths that run under WAL replay (the "redo" arms in dbcommands.c,
tablespace.c, and copydir.c) call the raw syscalls directly rather than
re-entering the FileOps* API.

Tests: src/test/recovery/t/062_fileops_recovery.pl (crash recovery),
063_fileops_2pc.pl (constructive-op 2PC via chmod),
064_fileops_standby_divergence.pl (physical-standby convergence),
061_fileops_2pc_deferred.pl (deferred commit-time ops through PREPARE +
crash), and the test_fileops module's regression test and TAP tests
(018_fileops_rename.pl covers FileOpsRename).
